Alembic migrations (async, asyncpg). Run as the schema-owning role:

    alembic upgrade head          # apply
    alembic downgrade -1          # roll back one revision
    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "short description"

Zero-downtime policy
====================

API and worker pods roll one at a time, so for a while old and new code run against the
same schema. Every migration must therefore be compatible with the code version before it
and the code version after it.

1. Expand, then contract. Additive changes (new table, new nullable column, new index)
   ship first. Code that stops using a column ships next. The drop ships in a later
   release, never in the same deploy as the code change.
2. Renames are add + backfill + switch reads + drop, across releases. Never use
   ALTER ... RENAME on a column that live code reads.
3. NOT NULL on an existing column: add it nullable, backfill in batches, add a
   `CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID` constraint, `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT`, then SET NOT NULL.
4. Indexes on large tables use `op.create_index(..., postgresql_concurrently=True)` inside
   `with op.get_context().autocommit_block():` (CONCURRENTLY cannot run in a transaction).
5. Keep locks short. Set `lock_timeout` (e.g. `SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '5s'`) in
   migrations that take ACCESS EXCLUSIVE locks, so a blocked migration fails fast instead
   of queueing every query behind it. Retry it off-peak.
6. No long data backfills inside a migration. Do them with a batched job (arq worker) or
   a separate script.
7. Every revision has a working downgrade(). `tests/test_migrations.py` runs
   up -> down -> up on a scratch database and checks that the models match the schema.
8. New org-scoped tables: enable RLS with a `tenant_isolation` policy and grant access to
   `corral_app` in the same migration that creates the table (see 0003).

Connection pool
===============

Each process holds up to DB_POOL_SIZE + DB_MAX_OVERFLOW connections (default 10 + 5).
Size it so that (api processes + worker processes) x (pool + overflow) stays below the
server's max_connections minus headroom for migrations and admin sessions. Put PgBouncer
(transaction mode) in front of the database before scaling past that. pool_pre_ping
drops dead connections after failovers. statement_timeout (DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS) and
the connect timeout bound every query.
